    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d338">

                <pb facs="0519" n="425" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg425"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Olney to Mr. MacVeagh.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-11-12"><hi rendition="#italic">November 12,
                        1896</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 210.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your No. 209, of the 22d
                    ultimo, and the Department’s No. 207, of the 6th instant, I have to acknowledge
                    the receipt of your No. 212, of the 26th ultimo, in regard to the case of
                    Vittorio Gardella, an Italian by origin, who, leaving his native land in
                    infancy, emigrated to the United States, resided here many years, became
                    naturalized, and, returning to Italy on a brief visit, leaving his family at his
                    residence in the United States, has been arrested and drafted into the Italian
                    army.</p>
                <p>Your action in bringing the matter personally to the attention of the Italian
                    minister of foreign affairs is commended and the result awaited.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard Olney.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
